 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup is served from a local embedded SQLite index stored on the kiosk. The index is private to this node; no other node opens it or knows its schema.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the snapshot's SQLite index in a staging location apart from the active index, so lookups never see partially imported records. Activate the staged index with a single atomic switch, only after signature, schema, uniqueness, and index build all succeed. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. Each lookup is an exact-match query on the serial-number key of the active index, opened read-only.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, built by each kiosk at import | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookup must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access, and both alternatives make lookups depend on a network service. SQLite gives predictable exact-match lookup with no service to run; the cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives, which stays inside this node. |
